\title{baux documentation}
\meta{toc}{false}

\p{baux is a derivative-free optimization library with a Rust core and a Python API. Define a parameter space, evaluate proposed points, and return objective values.}

\subtree{
  \title{Installation}
  \p{Install the Python package or add the Rust crate:}
  \pre{
pip install baux
cargo add baux
  }
  \p{Prebuilt Python 3.12–3.15 wheels support Linux x86-64 and macOS Apple Silicon.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Usage}
  \<div>[id]{quickstart}{
  \pre{
import baux
import numpy as np

bounds = np.array([[-2.0, 2.0], [-1.0, 3.0]])
state = baux.init(bounds, pipe=baux.enn(), seed=0)

def rosenbrock(x):
    return -(1.0 - x[:, 0]) ** 2 - 20.0 * (x[:, 1] - x[:, 0] ** 2) ** 2

for _ in range(10):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=4)
    values = rosenbrock(batch.points)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, values)

print("Rosenbrock max(-f):", round(float(state.best_y), 5), "at", np.round(state.best_x, 4))
  }
  }
  \p{baux maximizes values. This example negates the coupled 2D Rosenbrock valley so the optimizer ascends along the curved ridge toward (1, 1). Keep the state returned by \code{ask} and \code{tell} for the next call.}
  \p{The [quickstart](quickstart.html) explains the loop, and the [Explorer](explorer.html) runs examples directly in your browser. See the [optimizer reference](docs.html) for inputs and outputs.}
}

